151
00:07:40,441 --> 00:07:43,720
It's like the NASCAR pit crew of assembly.

152
00:07:43,721 --> 00:07:46,020
How often do these cycle through?

153
00:07:46,021 --> 00:07:48,280
We're normally under three minutes of cycle.

154
00:07:48,281 --> 00:07:49,120
Really?

155
00:07:49,121 --> 00:07:50,720
So three minutes start to finish?

156
00:07:50,721 --> 00:07:52,220
Crazy.

157
00:07:52,221 --> 00:07:55,020
The staggering efficiency of this assembly line

158
00:07:55,021 --> 00:07:57,200
has historic roots.

159
00:07:57,201 --> 00:07:59,500
The modern automotive assembly line

160
00:07:59,501 --> 00:08:02,800
began with the Ford Motor Company.

161
00:08:02,801 --> 00:08:06,140
We all know that Ford's concept was a moving production line

162
00:08:06,141 --> 00:08:08,940
where a car could be assembled one part at a time.

163
00:08:08,941 --> 00:08:12,480
But believe it or not, the inspiration for this idea

164
00:08:12,481 --> 00:08:16,480
came from Ford's observation of slaughterhouses.

165
00:08:16,481 --> 00:08:19,740
Ford went into these slaughterhouses

166
00:08:19,741 --> 00:08:23,180
and witnessed how these animals were dismembered.

167
00:08:23,181 --> 00:08:27,220
And he basically developed the Ford assembly line

168
00:08:27,221 --> 00:08:30,560
based on this disassembly of animals.

169
00:08:30,561 --> 00:08:33,960
Today, the technology, speed, and most of all size

170
00:08:33,961 --> 00:08:38,160
of these operations have grown.

171
00:08:38,161 --> 00:08:40,260
While the industry has scaled up,

172
00:08:40,261 --> 00:08:43,580
the core concept of an assembly line remains the same,

173
00:08:43,581 --> 00:08:48,020
including at Yamaha's massive operation.

174
00:08:48,021 --> 00:08:49,340
Adam, this is Anna.

175
