00:14:49,561 --> 00:14:53,100
as a fresh batch of trainees prepare for their first flight,

296
00:14:53,101 --> 00:14:56,400
starting with one who's taking off on foot.

297
00:14:56,401 --> 00:14:59,400
As a child growing up, you have dreams of flying,

298
00:14:59,401 --> 00:15:02,980
and so it's pretty cool to make it a reality.

299
00:15:02,981 --> 00:15:04,440
You ready for this?

300
00:15:04,441 --> 00:15:06,440
Training instructor Mike Gambrell

301
00:15:06,441 --> 00:15:09,380
goes through a pre-flight sequence with rookie pilot

302
00:15:09,381 --> 00:15:12,920
Jesse Sparks to make sure he's strapped securely

303
00:15:12,921 --> 00:15:14,120
into the machine.

304
00:15:14,121 --> 00:15:15,620
OK, radio check.

305
00:15:15,621 --> 00:15:16,460
All good?

306
00:15:16,461 --> 00:15:17,460
All good.

307
00:15:17,461 --> 00:15:18,460
OK.

308
00:15:18,461 --> 00:15:19,960
Mike will be in radio contact with Jesse

309
00:15:19,961 --> 00:15:21,460
for the entire flight.

310
00:15:21,461 --> 00:15:27,960
Today, Jesse will reach heights of up to 400 feet.

311
00:15:27,961 --> 00:15:29,840
All right, remember, we just want to concentrate

312
00:15:29,841 --> 00:15:31,300
on that good taxi, right?

313
00:15:31,301 --> 00:15:32,600
Yeah.

314
00:15:32,601 --> 00:15:35,400
And then we run into the air with a big grin on our face.

315
00:15:35,401 --> 00:15:37,180
OK?

316
00:15:37,181 --> 00:15:41,180
With the all clear from Mike, it's time to take off.

317
00:15:44,120 --> 00:15:48,120
OK, and whenever you're ready, use the motor

318
00:15:48,121 --> 00:15:50,620
to bring the glider up.

319
00:15:50,621 --> 00:15:54,120
A little more speed, a little more speed.

320
00:15:54,121 --> 00:15:55,120
There we go.

321
00:15:55,121 --> 00:15:58,120
And let's roll on the power.

322
00:15:58,121 --> 00:15:59,120
Run into the air.

323
00:15:59,121 --> 00:16:00,120
Run into the air.

324
00:16:00,121 --> 00:16:01,120
OK.

325
00:16:05,120 --> 00:16:07,120
As awe-inspiring as it is to watch,

326
00:16:07,121 --> 00:16:11,120
the process of controlling the flight is surprisingly easy.

327
00:16:11,121 --> 00:16:13,120
The hands control the glider.

328
00:16:13,121 --> 00:16:14,820
So if we want the glider to go left,

329
00:16:14,920 --> 00:16:16,920
we simply add a little bit of left brake.

330
00:16:17,020 --> 00:16:18,960
If we want to go right, we simply

331
00:16:19,060 --> 00:16:20,960
add a little bit of right brake.

332
00:16:21,060 --> 00:16:23,160
The throttle is merely for altitude.

333
00:16:23,260 --> 00:16:25,260
And if we want to go up, we're adding power.

334
00:16:25,360 --> 00:16:27,760
And if we want to come down, we're reducing power.

335
00:16:27,860 --> 00:16:31,180
These simple gestures create endless opportunities

336
00:16:31,280 --> 00:16:32,680
for aerial adventure.

337
00:16:32,780 --> 00:16:34,580
Experiencing flight with a paramotor

338
00:16:34,680 --> 00:16:37,580
is really unique to any other type of aviation.

339
00:16:37,680 --> 00:16:40,480
There's nothing in front of you, nothing but the breeze.

340
00:16:43,120 --> 00:16:45,580
That in itself sets it apart.

341
00:16:45,581 --> 00:16:48,660
You truly feel like Tom Cruise in Top Gun.

342
00:16:51,560 --> 00:16:54,460
While the drone camera filming our flyers today

343
00:16:54,461 --> 00:16:57,500
is up about 200 feet in the air,

344
00:16:57,501 --> 00:17:04,580
the legal limit for powered paragliders is 18,000 feet.

345
00:17:04,581 --> 00:17:07,040
Hold that heading right there is looking really nice.

346
00:17:07,041 --> 00:17:09,040
Oh!

347
00:17:09,041 --> 00:17:13,040
After about 15 minutes of flying a simple pattern

348
00:17:13,041 --> 00:17:16,040
above the field, Jesse uses the throttle

349
00:17:16,041 --> 00:17:20,040
to lower power to the engine, which reduces his altitude.

350
00:17:20,041 --> 00:17:21,040
How was it?

351
00:17:21,041 --> 00:17:22,040
Whoo!

352
00:17:22,041 --> 00:17:23,040
All right.

353
00:17:23,041 --> 00:17:25,040
Good job.

354
00:17:25,041 --> 00:17:26,040
Yeah!

355
00:17:32,040 --> 00:17:34,040
The answer is B.

356
00:17:34,041 --> 00:17:39,040
In 2019, three men from Brazil spent 11 hours in the air,

357
00:17:39,140 --> 00:17:44,180
paragliding just over 365 miles, about 100 miles longer

358
00:17:44,280 --> 00:17:46,860
than the width of Pennsylvania.

359
00:17:46,960 --> 00:17:52,960
Outdoor recreation accounts for an insane 2.1%

360
00:17:53,060 --> 00:17:56,060
of the American GDP, making it bigger

361
00:17:56,160 --> 00:17:59,160
than mining and agriculture.

362
00:17:59,260 --> 00:18:03,240
Over 80% of this includes both conventional activities

363
00:18:03,241 --> 00:18:08,440
activities like hiking, off-roading, water sports,

364
00:18:08,441 --> 00:18:12,680
and businesses supporting these types of activities.

365
00:18:12,681 --> 00:18:16,280
Yamaha is one of those businesses.

366
00:18:16,281 --> 00:18:18,680
The workers here use cutting-edge tech

367
00:18:18,681 --> 00:18:22,760
to create marvels like their Wolverine.

368
00:18:22,761 --> 00:18:24,760
Workers like Lazarus.

369
00:18:24,761 --> 00:18:25,960
Let me show you what we got.

370
00:18:25,961 --> 00:18:27,700
Please, all right, let's do it.

371
00:18:27,701 --> 00:18:31,240
Lazarus operates this state-of-the-art injection

372
00:18:31,241 --> 00:18:35,240
molding system that makes custom plastic parts for nearly every

373
00:18:35,241 --> 00:18:36,880
vehicle produced here.

374
00:18:36,881 --> 00:18:42,380
And right now, it's cooking up Wolverine's grill.

375
00:18:42,381 --> 00:18:45,880
This machine has got a, every cycle time, a 78 seconds.

376
00:18:45,881 --> 00:18:48,000
So every 78 seconds a new part's coming off?

377
00:18:48,001 --> 00:18:49,000
Every 78 seconds.

378
00:18:49,001 --> 00:18:51,100
When this mold cracks open, it pulls out a part.

379
00:18:51,101 --> 00:18:54,000
This is just insane.

380
00:18:54,001 --> 00:18:57,240
The molding process kicks off with a heating chamber

381
00:18:57,241 --> 00:19:03,040
containing liquid plastic that's kept at a searing 400 degrees.

382
00:19:03,140 --> 00:19:06,880
This chamber is connected to a machine for different molds.

383
00:19:06,980 --> 00:19:10,680
When, for example, a Wolverine's grill mold

384
00:19:10,780 --> 00:19:15,300
is inserted into the machine, the hot plastic gets shunted

385
00:19:15,400 --> 00:19:18,000
through the mold's injection ports.

386
00:19:18,100 --> 00:19:21,200
As this happens, water is constantly churning

387
00:19:21,300 --> 00:19:24,440
through the system to cool down the plastics

388
00:19:24,441 --> 00:19:28,680
as it settles into the mold and takes shape.

389
00:19:28,681 --> 00:19:32,240
It just boggles the mind because you look at a thing like this

390
00:19:32,241 --> 00:19:34,980
and you see this is like a NASA control system.

391
00:19:34,981 --> 00:19:38,380
So much engineering, so much technology

392
00:19:38,381 --> 00:19:40,920
to make just the grill for the ROV.

393
00:19:40,921 --> 00:19:41,760
This is amazing.

394
00:19:41,761 --> 00:19:43,300
That's right.

395
00:19:43,301 --> 00:19:45,620
Cutting edge tech is doing wonders,

396
00:19:45,621 --> 00:19:48,300
not just here at Yamaha.

397
00:19:48,301 --> 00:19:50,960
There's another company that's breaking new ground

398
00:19:50,961 --> 00:19:52,960
in the world of adventure machines

399
00:19:52,961 --> 00:19:56,960
by creating a futuristic technological wonder.

400
00:19:56,961 --> 00:20:00,300
This machine here is Prosthesis,

401
00:20:00,301 --> 00:20:04,300
the world's first purpose-built sports mech.

402
00:20:04,301 --> 00:20:07,300
It is a four-legged, human-piloted,

403
00:20:07,301 --> 00:20:11,120
all-electric powered exosuit.

404
00:20:11,121 --> 00:20:13,520
Exobionics founder Jonathan Tippett

405
00:20:13,521 --> 00:20:17,060
envisioned Prosthesis as the first in a class

406
00:20:17,061 --> 00:20:20,520
of massive exoskeletons that could compete against one

407
00:20:20,521 --> 00:20:24,020
another in racing and obstacle course events.

408
00:20:24,021 --> 00:20:27,160
These vehicles can't function without a person piloting it

409
00:20:27,161 --> 00:20:32,200
from inside its massive body, which weighs over four tons.

410
00:20:32,201 --> 00:20:34,160
We had made a machine so huge it had

411
00:20:34,161 --> 00:20:36,500
to have a ladder built into it.

412
00:20:36,501 --> 00:20:39,200
To get in, you drop in through here.

413
00:20:39,201 --> 00:20:41,200
And it's a tight squeeze on your way through.

414
00:20:41,201 --> 00:20:44,340
But once you get into position, you kind of expand

415
00:20:44,341 --> 00:20:46,840
into this body-shaped interface.

416
00:20:46,841 --> 00:20:49,380
And it engages your body in a number of locations.

417
00:20:49,381 --> 00:20:53,880
There's clamps on your legs, and then you clip in your feet.

418
00:20:53,881 --> 00:20:56,720
And there's clamps on your forearms,

419
00:20:56,721 --> 00:20:58,720
and then you grip the grips.

420
00:20:58,721 --> 00:21:01,300
So at this point, I can't move at all.

421
00:21:01,301 --> 00:21:03,520
I'm locked into the machine.

422
00:21:03,521 --> 00:21:07,240
Until the machine is powered up, I can't move anything.

423
00:21:07,241 --> 00:21:09,140
With just a flip of a switch,

424
00:21:09,141 --> 00:21:11,940
the pilot and mech become one.

425
00:21:19,380 --> 00:21:28,380
When this behemoth is turned on, two lithium-ion battery packs send power to two motors.

426
00:21:28,381 --> 00:21:36,380
The motors activate hydraulic cylinders that pump to move the legs.

427
00:21:36,381 --> 00:21:42,380
But how and where the four legs move is entirely up to the pilot.

428
00:21:42,381 --> 00:21:47,380
Each joint on the mech's legs is controlled by a corresponding joint on the pilot's body

429
00:21:47,381 --> 00:21:50,180
through an exoskeletal interface,

430
00:21:50,181 --> 00:21:52,680
which the pilot connects to at four points,

431
00:21:52,681 --> 00:21:57,620
the feet, the thighs, the arms, and the hands.

432
00:21:57,621 --> 00:22:01,220
A pilot uses their arms to control the two outer legs

433
00:22:01,221 --> 00:22:04,500
of the mech, while the two inner legs are controlled

434
00:22:04,501 --> 00:22:06,260
by the pilot's leg movement.

435
00:22:06,261 --> 00:22:09,460
So you are basically learning how to be a quadruped.

436
00:22:14,340 --> 00:22:17,380
There's a steep learning curve to readjusting your legs

437
00:22:17,381 --> 00:22:20,380
especially when you have a 4,000-kilogram body

438
00:22:20,381 --> 00:22:24,380
and a 200-horsepower heart.

439
00:22:24,381 --> 00:22:26,380
It's a little wild.

440
00:22:26,381 --> 00:22:29,380
At first, it's really stiff and really hard,

441
00:22:29,381 --> 00:22:30,380
really exhausting.

442
00:22:30,381 --> 00:22:33,380
You're using muscles that you don't need to all the time.

443
00:22:33,381 --> 00:22:36,380
But as you gain proficiency, once you get accustomed

444
00:22:36,381 --> 00:22:39,380
to the machine, it really floats.

445
00:22:39,381 --> 00:22:42,380
It's unbelievable that such a giant machine can feel so light

446
00:22:42,381 --> 00:22:44,380
in your hands when you get dialed in.

447
00:22:47,380 --> 00:22:48,880
Whoo!

448
00:22:48,881 --> 00:22:50,880
The journey we're on right now is figuring out

449
00:22:50,881 --> 00:22:52,720
what makes a good neck pilot.

450
00:22:52,721 --> 00:22:54,720
It's starting to become clear that, like,

451
00:22:54,721 --> 00:22:58,220
a large degree of body awareness, good balance,

452
00:22:58,221 --> 00:23:01,720
and we're finding that gymnasts,

453
00:23:01,721 --> 00:23:03,720
free runners who do parkour,

454
00:23:03,721 --> 00:23:06,140
we think maybe motocross riders will be pretty good,

455
00:23:06,141 --> 00:23:08,340
kite boarders, snowboarders.

456
00:23:08,341 --> 00:23:11,580
The vision we have is for, like, a giant obstacle course,

457
00:23:11,581 --> 00:23:13,680
like a stadium-sized parkour gym,

458
00:23:13,681 --> 00:23:16,340
where pro athletes are leaping and jumping and jumping

459
00:23:16,341 --> 00:23:19,100
and jumping and pushing obstacles and lifting and dragging.

460
00:23:19,180 --> 00:23:21,920
As much as we've been focused on the sports side of things,

461
00:23:21,980 --> 00:23:23,340
the thing just keeps coming in handy.

462
00:23:23,420 --> 00:23:25,920
I mean, we were out in the desert shooting the machine,

463
00:23:25,980 --> 00:23:29,980
and the truck that I was driving got stuck in the sand.

464
00:23:30,060 --> 00:23:32,180
So I towed the whole truck and trailer out of the sand

465
00:23:32,260 --> 00:23:34,100
using the mech.

466
00:23:34,160 --> 00:23:36,520
While research and development on prosthesis

467
00:23:36,600 --> 00:23:41,140
cost $2 million, the hope is to one day produce sports mechs

468
00:23:41,200 --> 00:23:43,500
that cost less than $100,000.

469
00:23:43,501 --> 00:23:47,500
The inspiration for this project was a vision I have of myself

470
00:23:47,501 --> 00:23:50,500
in a giant, agile, powered mech suit,

471
00:23:50,501 --> 00:23:53,000
bounding across a desert landscape,

472
00:23:53,001 --> 00:23:54,920
faster than a person can run,

473
00:23:54,921 --> 00:23:58,380
with the pumps whirring, dialed into the machine.

474
00:23:58,381 --> 00:23:59,920
That still drives me.

475
00:23:59,921 --> 00:24:01,380
You know, the sky's the limit.

476
00:24:01,381 --> 00:24:03,080
Like, this is a new class of vehicle.

477
00:24:03,081 --> 00:24:05,080
This will evolve for generations.

478
00:24:05,081 --> 00:24:08,500
This is the inception of the mech era.

479
00:24:08,501 --> 00:24:10,500
Whoo!

480
00:24:10,501 --> 00:24:13,500
Prosthesis is a vision for the future.

481
00:24:15,500 --> 00:24:18,500
But here in the present, I'm inching closer

482
00:24:18,501 --> 00:24:20,500
to a finished Wolverine.

